  "content": {
    "id": "p61-q18-content",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "18",
    "prompt": "Differentiate $\\ln(\\ln x)$ and hence determine the family of primitives of $\\dfrac{1}{x\\ln x}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\ln(\\ln x)=\\dfrac{1}{x\\ln x}$ for $x>1$.\n\nThe family of primitives is $\\ln|\\ln x|+C$, for $x>0$, $x\\ne1$.",
      "worked": "For $x>1$,\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln(\\ln x)&=\\frac{1}{\\ln x}\\times\\frac{1}{x}\\\\&=\\frac{1}{x\\ln x}.\\end{aligned}$$\nHence, on this domain,\n$$\\int\\frac{1}{x\\ln x}\\,dx=\\ln(\\ln x)+C.$$\nMore generally, the family of primitives is $\\ln|\\ln x|+C$ on either interval $0<x<1$ or $x>1$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
